// Build profile: TARGET HAS NO BLOCKING PRIMITIVES.
//
// The alternative to wb_dma_cfg_blocking.pss; see that file for what the flag
// selects and why it is phrased as "can the runtime suspend a thread" rather
// than "does the runtime have sync_pkg". Exactly one of the two is selected
// into a build.
//
// The intended target is low-level firmware: no scheduler, no interrupt
// dispatcher wired into PSS, no way to block a caller. Such a target gets the
// non-blocking core and NOTHING ELSE -- there is deliberately no end-to-end
// operation on this profile. `transfer_single_start()` arms the channel and
// returns; `check_completion()` reports PENDING / DONE / ERROR; the loop
// between them belongs to the firmware, which is the one place that knows what
// to do while waiting.
//
// Presenting the scenario layer (tests/pss) to this profile is a hard error,
// not a silent loss of actions -- see the compile assert in tests/pss/pss_top.pss.
package wb_dma_cfg_pkg {

    static const bool WB_DMA_HAS_BLOCKING = false;

}
